t affine since it contains the cubic term ${v}^{3}.$\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is affine. Notice that ${T}_{3}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u-2 \\\\ 8-v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3u \\\\ -v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{3}$ only.\""] [:db/cas 17592186259631 :question/problem "Which of the following are affine transformations?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}v-6u+7 \\\\ 4u+3v-5\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{2}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-7u-2v \\\\ 5u+v\\end{aligned}]$\n- ${T}_{3}:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2u+v \\\\ uv+1\\end{aligned}]$" "Which of the following are affine transformations?\n\n- ${T}_{1}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}v-6u+7 \\\\ 4u+3v-5\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{2}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-7u-2v \\\\ 5u+v\\end{bmatrix}$\n- ${T}_{3}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2u+v \\\\ uv+1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [:db/cas 17592186259631 :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is affine. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}v-6u+7 \\\\ 4u+3v-5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6u+v \\\\ 4u+3v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is affine. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-7u-2v \\\\ 5u+v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7u-2v \\\\ 5u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn fact, the transformation ${T}_{2}$ is a linear transformation.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{3}$ is not affine since it contains the cross term $uv.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"${T}_{1}$ and ${T}_{2}$ only.\"" "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{1}$ is affine. Notice that ${T}_{1}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}v-6u+7 \\\\ 4u+3v-5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6u+v \\\\ 4u+3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Transformation ${T}_{2}$ is affine. Notice that ${T}_{2}$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7u-2v \\\\ 5u+v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-7u-2v \\\\ 5u+v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{\\begin{b