-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}t-2 \\\\ 5-4t \\\\ 2t+\\frac{1}{7}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}t \\\\ -4t \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{7}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $(-2,5,\\frac{1}{7})$  and is parallel to the vector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262695 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 3t \\\\ -2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+t \\\\ -5+3t \\\\ 4-2t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ And finally, the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2+t \\\\ y=-5+3t \\\\ z=4-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 3t \\\\ -2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+t \\\\ -5+3t \\\\ 4-2t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ And finally, the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2+t \\\\ y=-5+3t \\\\ z=4-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262696 :question/worked-solution "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4t \\\\ 5t \\\\ -4t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3+4t \\\\ -3+5t \\\\ 5-4t\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ And finally, the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3+4t \\\\ y=-3+5t \\\\ z=5-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to find a direction vector that is parallel to the given line. We can use the vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{OQ}-\\overset{\\to }{OP} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vector equation of the line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4t \\\\ 5t \\\\ -4t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+4t \\\\ -3+5t \\\\ 5-4t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ And finally, the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=3+4t \\\\ y=-3+5t \\\\ z=5-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262703 :question/worked-solution "First, the vector equation of the line is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ -5t \\\\ 6t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2+t \\\\ 3-5t \\\\ 5+6t\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the parametric equations are: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+t \\\\ y=3-5t \\\\ z=5+6t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, the vector equation of the line is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -5t \\\\ 6t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+t \\\\ 3-5t \\\\ 5+6t\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the parametric equations are: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2+t \\\\ y=3-5t \\\\ z=5+6t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262704 :question/worked-solution "First, the vector equation of the line is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3t \\\\ 2t \\\\ 7t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7-3t \\\\ -11+2t \\\\ 3+7t\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the parametric equations are:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=7-3t \\\\ y=-11+2t \\\\ z=3+7t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, the vector equation of the line is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -11 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3t \\\\ 2t \\\\ 7t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7-3t \\\\ -11+2t \\\\ 3+7t\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Therefore, the parametric equations are:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=7-3t \\\\ y=-11+2t \\\\ z=3+7t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262705 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{3}=\\frac{y-7}{-1}=\\frac{z-(-6)}{-7},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(4,7,-6)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨3,-1,-7⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-4}{3}=\\frac{y-7}{-1}=\\frac{z-(-6)}{-7},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(4,7,-6)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨3,-1,-7⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262707 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-10}{7}=\\frac{y-(-8)}{-4}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{11},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(10,-8,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨7,-4,11⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=10+7t \\\\ y=-8-4t \\\\ z=-1+11t\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Remember that the Cartesian (canonical) equation of the straight line that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y},{v}_{z}⟩$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}}=\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}}=\\frac{z-{z}_{0}}{{v}_{z}}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nComparing the above to our given equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-10}{7}=\\frac{y-(-8)}{-4}=\\frac{z-(-1)}{11},\n$$\n\nwe find that our line passes through the point $P(10,-8,-1)$ and is parallel to $v=⟨7,-4,11⟩.$ Therefore, its vector equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+tv \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=10+7t \\\\ y=-8-4t \\\\ z=-1+11t\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262743 :question/problem "$[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=$" "$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186262743 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot (-1)+(-6)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12+4-12 \\\\ -4-5+4 \\\\ -2+4+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -4 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}6\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot (-1)+(-6)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-2)\\cdot 2+5\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12+4-12 \\\\ -4-5+4 \\\\ -2+4+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262746 :question/problem "Find $Mz$ if \n$M=[\\begin{aligned}s & t \\\\ -t & s\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$z=[\\begin{aligned}s \\\\ t\\end{aligned}].$" "Find $Mz$ if \n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}s & t \\\\ -t & s\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$z=\\begin{bmatrix}s \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262746 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Mz & =[\\begin{aligned}s & t \\\\ -t & s\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}s \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}s\\cdot s+t\\cdot t \\\\ (-t)\\cdot s+s\\cdot t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}+{t}^{2} \\\\ -st+st\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{s}^{2}+{t}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Mz & =\\begin{bmatrix}s & t \\\\ -t & s\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}s \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}s\\cdot s+t\\cdot t \\\\ (-t)\\cdot s+s\\cdot t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{s}^{2}+{t}^{2} \\\\ -st+st\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{s}^{2}+{t}^{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262747 :question/problem "Find $Qg$ if \n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}m & 2m \\\\ -m & n\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$g=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$" "Find $Qg$ if \n$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}m & 2m \\\\ -m & n\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$g=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262747 :question/worked-solution "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Qg & =[\\begin{aligned}m & 2m \\\\ -m & n\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}m\\cdot (-2)+2m\\cdot 3 \\\\ -m\\cdot (-2)+n\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2m+6m \\\\ 2m+3n\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4m \\\\ 2m+3n\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We multiply each row in the matrix by the column vector using the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Qg & =\\begin{bmatrix}m & 2m \\\\ -m & n\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}m\\cdot (-2)+2m\\cdot 3 \\\\ -m\\cdot (-2)+n\\cdot 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2m+6m \\\\ 2m+3n\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4m \\\\ 2m+3n\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262749 :question/problem "If $S=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand $T=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ then $ST=$" "If $S=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $T=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ then $ST=$"] [:db/cas 17592186262749 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ST & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot (-6) & (-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot (-4) \\\\ 2\\cdot 6+1\\cdot (-6) & 2\\cdot 6+1\\cdot (-4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4+12 & -4+8 \\\\ 12+(-6) & 12+(-4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n." "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ST & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{3} & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\text{ }\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot (-6) & (-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot 6+(-2)\\cdot (-4) \\\\ 2\\cdot 6+1\\cdot (-6) & 2\\cdot 6+1\\cdot (-4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4+12 & -4+8 \\\\ 12+(-6) & 12+(-4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n."] [:db/cas 17592186262750 :question/problem "If $L=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,$\nand\n$M=[\\begin{aligned}-36 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$\nthen $LM=$" "If $L=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$\nand\n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}-36 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$\nthen $LM=$"] [:db/cas 17592186262750 :question/worked-solution "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LM & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 3\\end{aligned}]\\text{ }[\\begin{aligned}-36 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-36)+7\\cdot 3 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4+7\\cdot 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-36)+3\\cdot 3 & \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18+21 & 2+7 \\\\ -9+9 & 1+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n." "Multiplying each row in the first matrix by each column in the second, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LM & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} & 7 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\text{ }\\begin{bmatrix}-36 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-36)+7\\cdot 3 & \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4+7\\cdot 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-36)+3\\cdot 3 & \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4+3\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-18+21 & 2+7 \\\\ -9+9 & 1+3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n."] [:db/cas 17592186262766 :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line."] [:db/cas 17592186262766 :question/worked-solution "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(-2,3,3)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨2,-5,3⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-2)}{2}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z-3}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z-3}{3}.\n$$" "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(-2,3,3)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨2,-5,3⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-2)}{2}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z-3}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+2}{2}=\\frac{y-3}{-5}=\\frac{z-3}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262767 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1-2t \\\\ 5-t \\\\ -7+t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ -t \\\\ t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(1,5,-7)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-2,-1,1⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z-(-7)}{1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z+7}{1}.\n$$" "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-2t \\\\ 5-t \\\\ -7+t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2t \\\\ -t \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(1,5,-7)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-2,-1,1⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z-(-7)}{1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-1}{-2}=\\frac{y-5}{-1}=\\frac{z+7}{1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262768 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2-6t \\\\ 1+3t \\\\ -5+2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-6t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(2,1,-5)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-6,3,2⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-6}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-(-5)}{2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-6}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z+5}{2}.\n$$" "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2-6t \\\\ 1+3t \\\\ -5+2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-6t \\\\ 3t \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(2,1,-5)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-6,3,2⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-6}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z-(-5)}{2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{-6}=\\frac{y-1}{3}=\\frac{z+5}{2}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262769 :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line." "Consider the straight line with the vector equation\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nfor $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ Find the Cartesian equation of this line."] [:db/cas 17592186262769 :question/worked-solution "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(7,-2,4)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨1,-2,3⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{1}=\\frac{y-(-2)}{-2}=\\frac{z-4}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{1}=\\frac{y+2}{-2}=\\frac{z-4}{3}.\n$$" "We have the vector equation \n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThis implies that the straight line passes through the point $P(7,-2,4)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨1,-2,3⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{1}=\\frac{y-(-2)}{-2}=\\frac{z-4}{3}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-7}{1}=\\frac{y+2}{-2}=\\frac{z-4}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262770 :question/worked-solution "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3-2t \\\\ -5t \\\\ 1-t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2t \\\\ -5t \\\\ -t\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ r & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(3,0,1)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-2,-5,-1⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-2}=\\frac{y-0}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-2}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}.\n$$" "First, let's find the corresponding vector equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3-2t \\\\ -5t \\\\ 1-t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2t \\\\ -5t \\\\ -t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ r & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nThis implies that the given line passes through the point $P(3,0,1)$ and is parallel to the vector $v=⟨-2,-5,-1⟩.$ So, its Cartesian (canonical) equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-2}=\\frac{y-0}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we reach\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-2}=\\frac{y}{-5}=\\frac{z-1}{-1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262771 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262771 :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{-2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3+3t \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1-2t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\\,y=2\n$$" "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{-2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{0}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y-2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=2:$\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-3+3t \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1-2t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y-2}{0}$ part and instead write $y=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{-2},\\,y=2\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262774 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262774 :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{7}=\\frac{y-5}{0}=\\frac{z}{2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{y-5}{0}=\\frac{z}{2},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y-5}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=5:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}-3+7t \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y-5}{0}$ part and instead write $y=5$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{z}{2},\\,y=5\n$$" "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-(-3)}{7}=\\frac{y-5}{0}=\\frac{z}{2}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{y-5}{0}=\\frac{z}{2},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{y-5}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $y$ component always maintains the constant value of $y=5:$\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}-3+7t \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{y-5}{0}$ part and instead write $y=5$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x+3}{7}=\\frac{z}{2},\\,y=5\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262775 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$" "Find the Cartesian equation for the line with the following vector equation:\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262775 :question/worked-solution "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-(-2)}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1}=\\frac{z+2}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z+2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=-2:$\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}3-5t \\\\ t \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z+2}{0}$ part and instead write $z=-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1},\\,z=-2\n$$" "Using the usual formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-(-2)}{0}=t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nor equivalently\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1}=\\frac{z+2}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is problematic because $\\frac{z+2}{0}$ involves division by zero.\n\nHowever, notice that in our line, the $z$ component always maintains the constant value of $z=-2:$\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}3-5t \\\\ t \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\nSo, in the Cartesian equation, we remove the $\\frac{z+2}{0}$ part and instead write $z=-2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x-3}{-5}=\\frac{y}{1},\\,z=-2\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262800 :question/problem "Calculate $det(C)$, if\n    $C=[\\begin{aligned}-x & -2y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & 5y\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate $det(C)$, if\n    $C=\\begin{bmatrix}-x & -2y \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & 5y\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262802 :question/problem "Calculate $det(D)$, if\n    $D=[\\begin{aligned}2x & y \\\\ -5 & xy\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate $det(D)$, if\n    $D=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & y \\\\ -5 & xy\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262803 :question/problem "If\n    $U=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 2k & -k+2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-1$, what is the value of $k?$" "If\n    $U=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 2k & -k+2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-1$, what is the value of $k?$"] [:db/cas 17592186262804 :question/problem "If\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}v & 2v \\\\ 2 & v+8\\end{aligned}]$\nand $det(T)=-4$, what is the value of $v?$" "If\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}v & 2v \\\\ 2 & v+8\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(T)=-4$, what is the value of $v?$"] [:db/cas 17592186262805 :question/problem "If\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}r-3 & r \\\\ 3 & r\\end{aligned}]$\nand $det(P)=-9$, what is the value of $r?$" "If\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}r-3 & r \\\\ 3 & r\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(P)=-9$, what is the value of $r?$"] [:db/cas 17592186262806 :question/problem "If\n$B=[\\begin{aligned}5-p & p \\\\ 3 & p\\end{aligned}]$\nand $det(B)=1$, what is the value of $p?$" "If\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}5-p & p \\\\ 3 & p\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(B)=1$, what is the value of $p?$"] [:db/cas 17592186262807 :question/problem "If\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3y & y \\\\ 3 & y-5\\end{aligned}]$\nand $det(A)=-27$, what is the value of $y?$" "If\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3y & y \\\\ 3 & y-5\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(A)=-27$, what is the value of $y?$"] [:db/cas 17592186262812 :question/problem "Calculate the minor of the entry ${d}_{23}$ in the matrix\n$D=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -12 & 44 & -33 \\\\ 25 & 30 & 41\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate the minor of the entry ${d}_{23}$ in the matrix\n$D=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 & 5 \\\\ -12 & 44 & -33 \\\\ 25 & 30 & 41\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262812 :question/worked-solution "The minor of ${d}_{23}=-33$ (second row, third column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $-33$ are removed: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & -33 \\\\ 25 & 30 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${d}_{23}=-33$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 \\\\ 25 & 30\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 \\\\ 25 & 30\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =10\\cdot 30-2\\cdot 25 \\\\  & =300-50 \\\\  & =250.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The minor of ${d}_{23}=-33$ (second row, third column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $-33$ are removed: \n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & -33 \\\\ 25 & 30 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${d}_{23}=-33$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 \\\\ 25 & 30\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}10 & 2 \\\\ 25 & 30\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =10\\cdot 30-2\\cdot 25 \\\\  & =300-50 \\\\  & =250.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262813 :question/problem "Calculate the minor of the entry ${p}_{31}$ in the matrix\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 7 & -9 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 72\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate the minor of the entry ${p}_{31}$ in the matrix\n$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-11 & 7 & -9 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 72\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262813 :question/worked-solution "The minor of ${p}_{31}=8$ (third row, first column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $8$ are removed: \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 7 & -9 \\\\ ∗ & -2 & 9 \\\\ 8 & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${p}_{31}=8$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =7\\cdot 9-(-9)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =63-18 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The minor of ${p}_{31}=8$ (third row, first column) is the determinant of the remaining entries when the column and row containing $8$ are removed: \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & 7 & -9 \\\\ ∗ & -2 & 9 \\\\ 8 & ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the minor of ${p}_{31}=8$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7\\cdot 9-(-9)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =63-18 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262820 :question/problem "Calculate ${B}^{-1}$, where \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate ${B}^{-1}$, where \n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262820 :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $B$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(B)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(B)=2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1=-1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(B)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B{B}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {B}^{-1}B & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $B$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(B)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(B)=2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1=-1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{B}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(B)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B{B}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {B}^{-1}B & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262821 :question/problem "Calculate ${C}^{-1}$, where\n$C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$" "Calculate ${C}^{-1}$, where\n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262821 :question/worked-solution "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $C$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(C)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(C)=3\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 3=-3\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(C)}[\\begin{aligned}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C{C}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {C}^{-1}C & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given a $2\\times 2$ matrix\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe inverse of $C$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(C)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\ndet(C)=3\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 3=-3\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{det(C)}\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C{C}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓ \\\\ {C}^{-1}C & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={I}_{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262822 :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262823 :question/problem "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262824 :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nS=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,U=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nS=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,T=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,U=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262825 :question/problem "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the matrices below does **not** have an inverse?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 12 \\\\ 3 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262826 :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262839 :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}k-1 & k-1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $k.$" "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}k-1 & k-1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262840 :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}k & 3 \\\\ 27 & k\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $k.$" "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ 27 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $k.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262841 :question/problem "If $A=[\\begin{aligned}48 & a \\\\ a & 3\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $a.$" "If $A=\\begin{bmatrix}48 & a \\\\ a & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $a.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262842 :question/problem "If $M=[\\begin{aligned}t & 5 \\\\ t & 9\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $t.$" "If $M=\\begin{bmatrix}t & 5 \\\\ t & 9\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $t.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262843 :question/problem "If $P=[\\begin{aligned}n & -4 \\\\ -16 & n\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $n.$" "If $P=\\begin{bmatrix}n & -4 \\\\ -16 & n\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $n.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262844 :question/problem "If $M=[\\begin{aligned}t & 2 \\\\ t & -8\\end{aligned}]$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $t.$" "If $M=\\begin{bmatrix}t & 2 \\\\ t & -8\\end{bmatrix}$ is a singular matrix, calculate the value of $t.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262850 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."] [:db/cas 17592186262851 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true."] [:db/cas 17592186262852 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."] [:db/cas 17592186262854 :question/problem "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n- **III.** The lines $x+3y=-1$ and $-x+ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point." "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -3.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $Ax=0$ has no solutions\n- **III.** The lines $x+3y=-1$ and $-x+ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point."] [:db/cas 17592186262854 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k+3\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to  $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot k-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =k+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $k+3\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -3.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to  $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement III is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements I and III are true."] [:db/cas 17592186262856 :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$\n- **III.** ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist" "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- **II.** $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$\n- **III.** ${A}^{-1}$ does **not** exist"] [:db/cas 17592186262856 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statements I and II are true." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =5\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the first part of the invertible matrix theorem, the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ must exist.\n\nFinally, only statements I and II are true."] [:db/cas 17592186262866 :question/problem "What is the minor of ${a}_{12}$ in the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3d & 5b \\\\ b & {c}^{2} & b-d \\\\ b & -10c & b+d\\end{aligned}]?$" "What is the minor of ${a}_{12}$ in the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3d & 5b \\\\ b & {c}^{2} & b-d \\\\ b & -10c & b+d\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186262866 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the first row and second column:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & -3d & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗ & b-d \\\\ b & ∗ & b+d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}b & b-d \\\\ b & b+d\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}b & b-d \\\\ b & b+d\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =b\\cdot (b+d)-(b-d)\\cdot b \\\\  & ={b}^{2}+bd-{b}^{2}+db \\\\  & =2bd\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we remove the first row and second column:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & -3d & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗ & b-d \\\\ b & ∗ & b+d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}b & b-d \\\\ b & b+d\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}b & b-d \\\\ b & b+d\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =b\\cdot (b+d)-(b-d)\\cdot b \\\\  & ={b}^{2}+bd-{b}^{2}+db \\\\  & =2bd\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262867 :question/problem "What is the minor of ${e}_{13}$ in the matrix\n$E=[\\begin{aligned}x & 2 & y \\\\ w-1 & 1-w & 3 \\\\ 1-w & w-1 & -10y\\end{aligned}]?$" "What is the minor of ${e}_{13}$ in the matrix\n$E=\\begin{bmatrix}x & 2 & y \\\\ w-1 & 1-w & 3 \\\\ 1-w & w-1 & -10y\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186262867 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the first row and the third column:\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ & y \\\\ w-1 & 1-w & ∗ \\\\ 1-w & w-1 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}w-1 & 1-w \\\\ 1-w & w-1\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}w-1 & 1-w \\\\ 1-w & w-1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(w-1)\\cdot (w-1)-(1-w)\\cdot (1-w) \\\\  & =(w-1)^{2}-(1-w)^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we remove the first row and the third column:\n\n$$\nE=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & ∗ & y \\\\ w-1 & 1-w & ∗ \\\\ 1-w & w-1 & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}w-1 & 1-w \\\\ 1-w & w-1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}w-1 & 1-w \\\\ 1-w & w-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(w-1)\\cdot (w-1)-(1-w)\\cdot (1-w) \\\\  & =(w-1)^{2}-(1-w)^{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262868 :question/problem "What is the minor of ${t}_{22}$ in the matrix\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}3p & r & 3p \\\\ q & r & -q \\\\ -6q & -p & 6q\\end{aligned}]?$" "What is the minor of ${t}_{22}$ in the matrix\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}3p & r & 3p \\\\ q & r & -q \\\\ -6q & -p & 6q\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186262868 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and the second column:\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3p & ∗ & 3p \\\\ ∗ & r & ∗ \\\\ -6q & ∗ & 6q\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det[\\begin{aligned}3p & 3p \\\\ -6q & 6q\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}3p & 3p \\\\ -6q & 6q\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3p\\cdot 6q-3p\\cdot (-6q) \\\\  & =18pq+18pq \\\\  & =36pq\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we remove the second row and the second column:\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3p & ∗ & 3p \\\\ ∗ & r & ∗ \\\\ -6q & ∗ & 6q\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det\\begin{bmatrix}3p & 3p \\\\ -6q & 6q\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}3p & 3p \\\\ -6q & 6q\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3p\\cdot 6q-3p\\cdot (-6q) \\\\  & =18pq+18pq \\\\  & =36pq\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262869 :question/problem "What is the minor of ${m}_{31}$ in the matrix\n$M=[\\begin{aligned}-50 & g & f \\\\ f & {g}^{2} & {f}^{2} \\\\ 20g & g & -5\\end{aligned}]?$" "What is the minor of ${m}_{31}$ in the matrix\n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}-50 & g & f \\\\ f & {g}^{2} & {f}^{2} \\\\ 20g & g & -5\\end{bmatrix}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186262869 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the third row and first column:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & g & f \\\\ ∗ & {g}^{2} & {f}^{2} \\\\ 20g & ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}g & f \\\\ {g}^{2} & {f}^{2}\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}g & f \\\\ {g}^{2} & {f}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =g\\cdot {f}^{2}-f\\cdot {g}^{2} \\\\  & =g{f}^{2}-f{g}^{2} \\\\  & =gf(f-g)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we remove the third row and first column:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & g & f \\\\ ∗ & {g}^{2} & {f}^{2} \\\\ 20g & ∗ & ∗\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}g & f \\\\ {g}^{2} & {f}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}g & f \\\\ {g}^{2} & {f}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =g\\cdot {f}^{2}-f\\cdot {g}^{2} \\\\  & =g{f}^{2}-f{g}^{2} \\\\  & =gf(f-g)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262870 :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${p}_{11}$ is $4$, find all the possible values of $t$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-5t & 18 & 6t \\\\ -3 & 9 & 5 \\\\ 19t & 1 & 3t\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Given that the minor of ${p}_{11}$ is $4$, find all the possible values of $t$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-5t & 18 & 6t \\\\ -3 & 9 & 5 \\\\ 19t & 1 & 3t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262870 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the first row and first column:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}5t & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 9 & 5 \\\\ ∗ & 1 & 3t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${p}_{11}$ is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3t\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =9\\cdot 3t-5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =27t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${p}_{11}$ is $4$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27t-5 & =4 \\\\ 27t & =9 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t=\\frac{1}{3}.$" "First, we remove the first row and first column:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}5t & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 9 & 5 \\\\ ∗ & 1 & 3t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${p}_{11}$ is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3t\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 3t-5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =27t-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${p}_{11}$ is $4$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27t-5 & =4 \\\\ 27t & =9 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262871 :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${d}_{12}$ is $-4$, find all the possible values of $p$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}{p}^{2} & {p}^{3} & 6 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2+p & p-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Given that the minor of ${d}_{12}$ is $-4$, find all the possible values of $p$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}{p}^{2} & {p}^{3} & 6 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 2+p & p-1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262871 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the first row and second column:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & {p}^{3} & ∗ \\\\ -6 & ∗ & 5 \\\\ 5 & ∗ & p-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${d}_{12}$ is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 5 \\\\ 5 & p-1\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}-6 & 5 \\\\ 5 & p-1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-6\\cdot (p-1)-5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-6p-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${d}_{12}$ is $-4$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6p-19 & =-4 \\\\ -6p & =15 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p=-\\frac{5}{2}.$" "First, we remove the first row and second column:\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & {p}^{3} & ∗ \\\\ -6 & ∗ & 5 \\\\ 5 & ∗ & p-1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${d}_{12}$ is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 5 \\\\ 5 & p-1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 5 \\\\ 5 & p-1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot (p-1)-5\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-6p-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${d}_{12}$ is $-4$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6p-19 & =-4 \\\\ -6p & =15 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p=-\\frac{5}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262872 :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${m}_{21}$ is $11$, find all the possible values of $y$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}y & \\frac{3}{2} & y \\\\ 25 & 4-y & 12y \\\\ y & 2 & -6y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Given that the minor of ${m}_{21}$ is $11$, find all the possible values of $y$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}y & \\frac{3}{2} & y \\\\ 25 & 4-y & 12y \\\\ y & 2 & -6y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262872 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the second row and first column:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & \\frac{3}{2} & y \\\\ 25 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 2 & -6y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${m}_{21}$ is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & y \\\\ 2 & -6y\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & y \\\\ 2 & -6y\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-6y)-y\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-11y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${m}_{21}$ is $11$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-11y & =11 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y=-1.$" "First, we remove the second row and first column:\n\n$$\nM=\\begin{bmatrix}∗ & \\frac{3}{2} & y \\\\ 25 & ∗ & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & 2 & -6y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${m}_{21}$ is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & y \\\\ 2 & -6y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{3}{2} & y \\\\ 2 & -6y\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-6y)-y\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-11y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${m}_{21}$ is $11$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-11y & =11 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y=-1.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262875 :question/problem "Given that the minor of ${r}_{33}$ is $3$, find all the possible values of $z$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}z & -3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & z-8 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 6z\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Given that the minor of ${r}_{33}$ is $3$, find all the possible values of $z$ in the matrix\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}z & -3 & -1 \\\\ 5 & z-8 & 8 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 6z\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262875 :question/worked-solution "First, we remove the third row and third column:\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}z & -3 & ∗ \\\\ -5 & z-8 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 6z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${r}_{33}$ is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}[\\begin{aligned}z & -3 \\\\ 5 & z-8\\end{aligned}] & =|\\begin{aligned}z & -3 \\\\ 5 & z-8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =z\\cdot (z-8)-(-3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & ={z}^{2}-8z+15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${r}_{33}$ is $3$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-8z+15 & =3 \\\\ {z}^{2}-8z+12 & =0 \\\\ (z-2)(z-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=2$ and $z=6.$" "First, we remove the third row and third column:\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}z & -3 & ∗ \\\\ -5 & z-8 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗ & 6z\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe minor of ${r}_{33}$ is\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}\\begin{bmatrix}z & -3 \\\\ 5 & z-8\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{vmatrix}z & -3 \\\\ 5 & z-8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =z\\cdot (z-8)-(-3)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & ={z}^{2}-8z+15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the minor of ${r}_{33}$ is $3$, so we must have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-8z+15 & =3 \\\\ {z}^{2}-8z+12 & =0 \\\\ (z-2)(z-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $z=2$ and $z=6.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262876 :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** $A$ is invertible\n- **III.** $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$" "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** $A$ is invertible\n- **III.** $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b$"] [:db/cas 17592186262876 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements II and III are true." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is true by the second part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nFinally, only statements II and III are true."] [:db/cas 17592186262877 :question/problem "Let $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -4.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** The system  $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- **III.** The lines $7x+14y=2$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at infinitely many points" "Let $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 14 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix},$ where $k$ is a constant such that $k\\ne -4.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(A)=0$\n- **II.** The system  $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n- **III.** The lines $7x+14y=2$ and $-2x+ky=-3$ intersect at infinitely many points"] [:db/cas 17592186262877 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =7\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =7k+28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $7k+28\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -4.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement II is true." "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 14 \\\\ -2 & k\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =7\\cdot k-(-2)\\cdot 14 \\\\  & =7k+28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $7k+28\\ne 0$ if $k\\ne -4.$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is the trivial one (zero).\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the lines must intersect at exactly one point.\n\nFinally, only statement II is true."] [:db/cas 17592186262879 :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the system $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]x=0$ has the unique zero solution, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n- **I.** ${[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}$ does not exist\n- **II.** The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n- **III.** $det[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$\n\nSelect all that apply." "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the system $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}x=0$ has the unique zero solution, then $\\text{__________}$ . *\n- **I.** ${\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}$ does not exist\n- **II.** The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n- **III.** $det\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0$\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186262879 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option III gives us a true statement:  \n\n*If the system $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]x=0$ has the unique zero solution, then, $det[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$" "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option III gives us a true statement:  \n\n*If the system $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}x=0$ has the unique zero solution, then, $det\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$"] [:db/cas 17592186262880 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and II give us true statements: \n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.* $\\,✓$" "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the first part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($A$ is invertible), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and II give us true statements: \n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If $A$ is invertible, then The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.* $\\,✓$"] [:db/cas 17592186262881 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$ intersect at one point, then \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has \n the unique solution $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$*" "Recall that given $A=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $-x+y=1$ and $x+ky=1$ intersect at one point, then \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If the lines intersect at one point, then $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & k\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has \n the unique solution $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186262900 :question/problem "Find the value of $(2f(t)+5g(t))^{′}$ at $t=1,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3+t} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1) \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2t-3 \\\\ {t}^{2} \\\\ (t+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Find the value of $(2f(t)+5g(t))^{′}$ at $t=1,$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{3+t} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1) \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2t-3 \\\\ {t}^{2} \\\\ (t+1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262900 :question/worked-solution "By the sum rule, we have \n \n\n$$\n(2f(t)+5g(t))^{′}=2{f}^{′}(t)+5{g}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(2\\sqrt{3+t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3+t}} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1)^{2})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 2(t+1)\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence , we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2f(1)+5g(1))^{′} & =2{f}^{′}(1)+5{g}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3+1}} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-1) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2(1) \\\\ 2(1+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "By the sum rule, we have \n \n\n$$\n(2f(t)+5g(t))^{′}=2{f}^{′}(t)+5{g}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\,(2\\sqrt{3+t}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3+t}} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {g}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t+1)^{2})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2t \\\\ 2(t+1)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence , we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2f(1)+5g(1))^{′} & =2{f}^{′}(1)+5{g}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3+1}} \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1-1) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2(1) \\\\ 2(1+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+5\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}11 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262901 :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-2,3⟩,\\,p=⟨3,-1,4⟩,$ and $q=⟨-1,0,1⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,4⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-4i+2j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-4,2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =⟨3,-1,4⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =10 \\\\ -4x+2y+6z & =10 \\\\ -4x+2y+6z-10 & =0 \\\\ 4x-2y-6z+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-2,3⟩,\\,p=⟨3,-1,4⟩,$ and $q=⟨-1,0,1⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,4⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4i+2j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-4,2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =⟨3,-1,4⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =10 \\\\ -4x+2y+6z & =10 \\\\ -4x+2y+6z-10 & =0 \\\\ 4x-2y-6z+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262904 :question/problem "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $S(1,-3,4)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$  and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$" "Find a parametric equation of the plane that passes through the point $S(1,-3,4)$ and is parallel to the vectors ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$  and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262904 :question/worked-solution "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\nr=p+sb+tc,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $b$ and $c$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $S$ is $p=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$, and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$  and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\nr=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+s[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\nr=p+sb+tc,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $b$ and $c$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $S$ is $p=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$, and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$  and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262905 :question/problem "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $u(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$" "Find $(u(t)f(t))^{′}$ where $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262905 :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)f(t))^{′}={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${f}^{′}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)f(t))^{′} & ={u}^{′}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{sin}^{2}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t-\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262906 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(Q)=-5$\n- **II.** $det(Q)=1$\n- **III.** $Q$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $Q$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $det(Q)=-5$\n- **II.** $det(Q)=1$\n- **III.** $Q$ is **not** a singular matrix\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262908 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(T)=-30$\n\n \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $T$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $T$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $T$ is **not** a singular matrix\n- **III.** $det(T)=-30$\n\n \n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262913 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(R)=0$\n- **III.** $det(R)=1$\n\n \n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $R$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $R$ is a singular matrix\n- **II.** $det(R)=0$\n- **III.** $det(R)=1$\n\n \n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262915 :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-3,4,1⟩$ and $w=⟨5,1,-2⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-9i-j-23k \\\\  & =⟨-9,-1,-23⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-9,-1,-23⟩.$" "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-3,4,1⟩$ and $w=⟨5,1,-2⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-9i-j-23k \\\\  & =⟨-9,-1,-23⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-9,-1,-23⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262916 :question/worked-solution "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-2,8,0⟩$ and $w=⟨3,-1,-1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-8i-2j-22k \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2,-22⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-8,-2,-22⟩.$" "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-2,8,0⟩$ and $w=⟨3,-1,-1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-8i-2j-22k \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2,-22⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-8,-2,-22⟩.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262917 :question/problem "Given that\n$B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ y & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand $det(B)=12,$ find the value of $y.$" "Given that\n$B=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ y & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nand $det(B)=12,$ find the value of $y.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262918 :question/problem "Given that \n$C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & q & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ \nand $det(C)=28,$ find the value of $q.$" "Given that \n$C=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & q & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ \nand $det(C)=28,$ find the value of $q.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262924 :question/problem "If $f(u)=[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ {u}^{2} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $u(t)={e}^{t}$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$" "If $f(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ {u}^{2} \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)={e}^{t}$, then $(f∘u)^{′}(t)=$"] [:db/cas 17592186262924 :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\,({u}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(6)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2u \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2u(t) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2({e}^{t}) \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(f∘u)^{′}(t)={f}^{′}(u(t)){u}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\,({u}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(6)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2u \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f∘u)^{′}(t) & =f′(u(t))u′(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2u(t) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {u}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2({e}^{t}) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186262936 :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(1,2,-3)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=0.$" "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(1,2,-3)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $(r-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186262936 :question/worked-solution "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(1,-2,1).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|0\\cdot (-2)+(-4)\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2|}{\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-4+8|}{\\sqrt{4+1+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|4|}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let's pick a point on the plane, for instance, $Q(1,-2,1).$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, from the equation of the plane, we get a normal vector $n=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to t